      "explanation": "<p>The Gabriel phthalimide synthesis is a method used to prepare primary aliphatic and aromatic amines. The process begins with phthalimide being treated with ethanolic potassium hydroxide (KOH), forming the potassium salt of phthalimide. This salt is then heated with an alkyl halide (RX), and subsequent treatment with either alkaline hydrolysis or hydrazinolysis (using hydrazine, <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.339ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"10.397ex\" height=\"1.885ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 4595.6 833\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"48\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-48\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(783,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(1186.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4E\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(2158.8,0)\"><use data-c=\"22C5\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-22C5\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(2659,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4E\"></use><use data-c=\"48\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-48\" transform=\"translate(750,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1533,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) yields the corresponding primary amine. However, this method cannot be used to synthesize primary aromatic amines that have electron-withdrawing groups (EWGs) at the ortho- and para-positions, such as the nitro group (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.339ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.205ex\" height=\"1.934ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 2742.6 855\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"msub\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4E\"></use><use data-c=\"4F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4F\" transform=\"translate(750,0)\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(1561,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>). This limitation arises because aryl halides (ArX) do not undergo nucleophilic substitution (<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.339ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.646ex\" height=\"1.934ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 1169.3 855\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"53\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-53\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(589,-150) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4E\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>) reactions with the anion formed by phthalimide.</p>\n<img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1lx51mwlw/f7cd1021-f008-4616-8173-72a7d05c1503/12d40540-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51mwlx.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/6y3zli1lx51mwlw/f7cd1021-f008-4616-8173-72a7d05c1503/12d40540-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51mwlx.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;max-height:40vh;vertical-align:baseline\" alt=\"JEE Main 2020 (Online) 6th September Evening Slot Chemistry - Compounds Containing Nitrogen Question 189 English Explanation 1\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/6y3zli1lx51mwlw/f7cd1021-f008-4616-8173-72a7d05c1503/12d40540-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51mwlx.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/6y3zli1lx51mwlw/f7cd1021-f008-4616-8173-72a7d05c1503/12d40540-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51mwlx.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1lx51mwlw/f7cd1021-f008-4616-8173-72a7d05c1503/12d40540-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51mwlx.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/6y3zli1lx51mwlw/f7cd1021-f008-4616-8173-72a7d05c1503/12d40540-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51mwlx.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/6y3zli1lx51mwlw/f7cd1021-f008-4616-8173-72a7d05c1503/12d40540-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51mwlx.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/6y3zli1lx51mwlw/f7cd1021-f008-4616-8173-72a7d05c1503/12d40540-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51mwlx.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\">\n<img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1lx51msd9/f8812fae-752a-412e-8e82-95183b211195/0f8d65c0-24ff-11ef-b38f-3f74eac790d6/file-6y3zli1lx51